/-
Cell-local kernel proof for SCR-040 — the loop-to-fold bridge at arbitrary
item count.

This file is evidence for the cell
`spec/equiv/cells/limits/C-L-scr-040-loop-fold` only.  It is NOT imported by
`spec/proofs/**` and it does not modify any shared Lean file.

## What this file is about

Bitcoin Core computes the BIP342 tapscript validation-weight seed as

    interpreter.cpp:1991-1992
      execdata.m_validation_weight_left =
          ::GetSerializeSize(witness.stack) + VALIDATION_WEIGHT_OFFSET;

with `VALIDATION_WEIGHT_OFFSET = 50` (script.h:62-65).  For
`witness.stack : std::vector<std::vector<unsigned char>>` the byte count is
produced by

    serialize.h:666-676  VectorFormatter::Ser
      Formatter formatter;
      WriteCompactSize(s, v.size());
      for (const typename V::value_type& elem : v)
          formatter.Ser(s, elem);

where each element goes through the BasicByte arm of the vector serializer

    serialize.h:900-902
      WriteCompactSize(os, v.size());
      if (!v.empty()) os.write(MakeByteSpan(v));

and every write is counted by `SizeComputer` (serialize.h:1121-1130,
1151-1154, 1156-1160).

The wave65 cell proved the *bounded* IR loop (counts 0..8) against the byte
count and proved the arbitrary-list identity on the model side, but left
"the C++ element loop is the Lean fold" as a reviewed prose premise.  This
file machine-checks that bridge for an arbitrary item count:

  * the pinned source fragments are embedded as string literals and the
    kernel checks (by `decide`) that each literal is the pinned text, and
    that the pretty-printed AST is character-identical to it modulo
    whitespace (`sameCode`);
  * a faithful small-step model of the loop is given, with an explicit index,
    the `i < size` guard, and the increment (not a `foldl` in disguise);
  * `runLoop_eq_fold` proves, by induction on the fuel, that this loop from
    index 0 reaches exactly the left fold of the per-element effect, for an
    arbitrary `List ByteArray` — no arity hypothesis;
  * `runI_innerProg` proves the per-element effect is
    `csSize(len) + len` with the `if (!v.empty())` guard discharged;
  * `seed_eq_validationWeightInit` proves the full seed (count prefix + item
    fold + the 50 parsed out of the pinned `VALIDATION_WEIGHT_OFFSET`
    initializer) equals the live btc-lean model definition
    `Bitcoin.Crypto.Taproot.Program.validationWeightInit`.

The residual premise, stated in spec.md and notes.md, is the *semantics of the
C++ fragment* (range-for over a vector, `size_t` accumulation, SizeComputer
write/seek, and the 1:1 transcription of the pinned text into the AST printed
by `renderO`/`renderI`).  It is no longer a claim about the program
(`loop = foldl`), which is what is proved here.
-/
import Bitcoin.Interpreter.Defs
import Bitcoin.Serialization.Interface

set_option maxRecDepth 100000

namespace Wave66.Scr040LoopFold

open Bitcoin.Data Bitcoin.Serialization
open Bitcoin.Crypto.Taproot.Program

/-! ## 1. Text normalisation

Whitespace is not a C++ token separator here beyond separating identifiers, and
the pinned fragments are free of adjacency hazards, so the source check is
"equal after deleting whitespace".  `decide` reduces it in the kernel. -/

/-- C++ whitespace (space, tab, LF, CR). -/
def isSpace (c : Char) : Bool :=
  c == ' ' || c == '\t' || c == '\n' || c == '\r'

/-- The characters of `s` with all whitespace removed. -/
def deSpace (s : String) : List Char := s.toList.filter (fun c => !isSpace c)

/-- Character-identical modulo whitespace. -/
def sameCode (a b : String) : Bool := deSpace a == deSpace b

